	<section>
		<h2>What is Piguaquan?</h2>
		<p>Piguaquan is a Chinese martial art that uses the whole body on an especially large scale. Its arms extend far outward, and coordinated full-body movement generates power.</p>
		<p>The arms are used at full length, but the power does not come from the arms alone. It travels through the feet, waist, back, shoulders, and arms to produce whole-body force.</p>
		<p>Piguaquan is well suited to attacks from a longer distance. Its expansive, outward-reaching movements are part of its appeal.</p>
		<p><i>Pi</i> refers to a movement that chops downward from above; <i>gua</i> describes a hooking motion. Piguaquan's appeal lies beyond the names of its techniques: it uses broad whole-body movements to transmit power into the arms.</p>
		<p>Piguaquan is often described as an old system of Chinese martial arts with connections to the Cangzhou area of Hebei Province. Related references are said to appear in martial arts writings from the Ming dynasty, and in modern times the art has been transmitted and organized in areas such as Cangzhou.</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Features of Piguaquan</h2>
		<p>A defining feature of Piguaquan is the supple connection of the feet, waist, back, shoulders, and arms. Its expansive movements involve speed, circular motion, flexibility, changes of direction, and the transfer of force.</p>
		<p>Its footwork is lively and winding rather than straight, while the body undulates like a dragon. Connecting the steps with the trunk creates Piguaquan's broad, supple movement.</p>
		<p>Among Chinese martial arts, Piguaquan is one of the styles with especially intense, difficult, and beautiful movement. Learning the motions and making them work in the body takes effort, but the sense of release when you begin to move well is exceptional.</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Large movements that use the arms at full length</li>
			<li>Quick movement and changes of direction</li>
			<li>Supple actions using circular movement</li>
			<li>Footwork that winds and weaves like a snake</li>
			<li>Body movement that undulates like a dragon</li>
			<li>Body mechanics connecting waist, back, and shoulders</li>
			<li>Movement that uses longer range</li>
			<li>Switching between opening and closing actions</li>
			<li>A balance of flexibility and striking power</li>
			<li>Energetic full-body movement that leaves you feeling refreshed</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Its dramatic appearance and intensity draw attention, but Piguaquan is also about learning how to connect the entire body. Beginners can enjoy it as exercise, while experienced practitioners can explore its body mechanics in depth.</p>
	</section>

